## 2026-09-08 — tick: ADVANCED (11) — at140 PASSED cycle 2; AT-147/148 built same tick
at140 closed checked-PASS cycle 2 (verdict 5253352). Checker verified all four fixes by execution
and corrected me IN MY FAVOUR twice: sabotage K now fails TWO tests, not one (the new AT-144 test
also trips), and all FOUR bound flags are pinned, not the two I claimed -- it drifted max_screens,
max_depth, max_actions and wall_clock_s independently and each broke something.
It also hit my own cycle-1 lesson itself: its first two AT-144 sabotages anchored on
`max_actions=max_actions`, which matches TWICE in the file, so the patch silently applied nothing
and reported 637 green. It caught that before believing it. Same trap, both directions, same day.
Then it filed two fresh findings on the new code, and I took them the SAME tick rather than
queueing them, because AT-147 is high and lands on the exact date T-145's grant would use:
AT-147 -- `_validate_grant` compared `< date.today()` while `is_expired` reads a bare date through
fromisoformat, i.e. MIDNIGHT. So `--expires <today>` was GRANTED with a green line and refused at
run time as expired. One rule, two comparisons, one whole day of disagreement.
Fixed at the GRANT and deliberately NOT by making is_expired inclusive: end-of-day is the more
intuitive reading, and adopting it WIDENS EVERY APPROVAL ALREADY ON DISK BY UP TO 24 HOURS.
Silently lengthening a consent window is not a change a maker makes to a security gate on its own
judgement -- put to the checker as a design question instead, with the note that if it prefers
end-of-day my conservative refusal should be REPLACED, not layered on.
AT-148 -- startswith(base_url) reads https://demo.test.evil.com/ as matching https://demo.test/.
Bounded (CN5 matches exactly at run time, nothing widens) but a typo advisory silent on precisely
the shape a typo-squat takes is not much of an advisory. Scheme+netloc equality before any path
prefix; a genuine sub-path still passes unflagged, because a warning that cries wolf on the normal
case gets ignored when it matters.
